        tuningPotential: [ { stage: "Stage 1", power: "300 - 310 hp", notes: "A software-only remap provides the most significant 'bang for buck' on the CVJA. It adds roughly 100Nm of torque, making the Q7 feel substantially more effortless in traffic while improving MPG under light cruise conditions.", zeroToHundred: "6.4s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" }, { stage: "Stage 2", power: "320 - 340 hp", notes: "Requires a high-flow downpipe and an upgraded intercooler. This stage significantly widens the torque band and allows the turbo to breathe much better at high RPMs, though it increases thermal stress on the plastic cooling system.", zeroToHundred: "6.0s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" }, { stage: "Stage 3 (IS38 Turbo)", power: "380 - 410 hp", notes: "The ultimate transformation for the 2.0T Q7. Swapping the factory turbo for the larger IS38 unit from the Golf R turns this SUV into a genuine sleeper, easily outrunning stock V6 models while maintaining 4-cylinder efficiency.", zeroToHundred: "5.4s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" } ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 252, nm: 370 }, stage1: { hp: 305, nm: 470 }, stage2: { hp: 330, nm: 510 }, stage3: { hp: 400, nm: 580 } },
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/audi/Q7/4M/edited.glb", model3DScale: 0.011, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.87, y: 0.08, z: 2.36 } }, riskRating: 5, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.35, headlightIntensity: 200, headlightOpacity: 1, headlightRadiusTop: 0.04, headlightTargetDistance: 74.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.2, meshGlowHeadlight: 10, meshGlowTaillight: 13, meshGlowDrl: 8, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: {}, model3DHotspots: [], customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillightsfix"], drllight: ["drlfix"] }, author: "2015_audi_q7 (https://skfb.ly/oTPoz) by Jean Wally's™ is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
        drivingExperience: "Driving a 4-cylinder engine in a massive luxury SUV like the Q7 4M is a surprisingly pleasant experience that defies initial skepticism. Thanks to the massive weight reduction of the 4M platform and the instant low-end boost of the EA888 Gen 3, the 252hp CVJA feels remarkably nimble and punchy around town, masking its small displacement with clever gearing. The lack of a heavy V6 or V8 over the front axle significantly improves the vehicle's turn-in and steering precision, making it feel less like a boat and more like a high-riding wagon. While it lacks the effortless, low-decibel surge and 'prestige' sound of the 3.0 TDI or TFSI variants during high-speed highway overtakes, it remains exceptionally refined and quiet. It is the perfect choice for urban-dwelling owners who prioritize agility, lower insurance brackets, and modern efficiency over raw towing capacity and high-speed locomotive-style cruising.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The CVJA is a technician's favorite for access but a constant source of income due to its peripheral frailties. The EA888 Gen 3 is a masterpiece of small-displacement engineering, being far more reliable than its predecessors, but it is still 'Audi-fragile' in the cooling department. You will inevitably replace the plastic water pump housing and thermostat every 80-100k kilometers, and the turbocharger wastegate rattle is a frequent visitor on high-mileage examples. However, compared to the engine-out nightmares of the V8s and V12s, the 2.0T is a breeze to work on; everything is accessible, and the parts are relatively affordable. It is a robust engine that demands a 'preventative' owner—one who swaps the plastic cooling parts for aluminum before they leak and blasts the intake valves before misfires begin. If you stay ahead of the carbon and the coolant, this 4-cylinder will reliably haul this heavy chassis for many years.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$800 - $1,800 / year (Generally much lower than the V6/V8 models, but the frequent cooling system failures and mandatory carbon cleaning keep maintenance costs firmly in the premium segment)."
